Scott defines an approximable map \(f\) between recursively presented domains to be computable when \(X_n\, f\, Y_m\) is recursively enumerable in the indices, and explains that the one-point domain specialises this to a computable element \(y\), the set \(\{m \mid Y_m \in y\}\) being recursively enumerable, recursive when \(y\) is finite and in general only listable when \(y\) is infinite. `ComputableMap` states the graph clause: relative to computable presentations, an approximable map is computable when the relation on indices \(n,m\) holding exactly when the \(n\)th neighbourhood is related to the \(m\)th is recursively enumerable. `ComputableElement` states that \(y\) is computable when the set of indices whose enumerated neighbourhood lies in \(y\) is recursively enumerable. Beyond the definition, `idMap_isComputable` shows the identity is computable because its relation is inclusion of enumerated neighbourhoods, which the presentation decides, and a decidable predicate is recursively enumerable; `comp_isComputable` shows a composite of computable maps is computable by reindexing the graphs so one code presents a domain index against a middle index and the other presents that middle index against a codomain index, conjoining them, and projecting away the middle index, with surjectivity of the middle presentation passing between a witnessing neighbourhood and an index; `apply_isComputableElement` uses the same reindex-conjoin-project pattern on the graph and the index set of a computable element, from the equivalence that a codomain neighbourhood lies in the image exactly when some domain neighbourhood lies in the element and is related to it; `principal_isComputableElement` shows the principal element of the \(n\)th neighbourhood is computable, and that membership is decidable, by pairing the fixed index \(n\) with a variable index and composing with decidable inclusion together with the presentation's neighbourhood predicate. That decidability is Scott's finite case for principal elements. The primitive-recursive listing of a general recursively enumerable filter is not proved here, and nothing in the four closure proofs contradicts the two definitional clauses. **Theorem:** yes — `ComputableMap` and `ComputableElement` are Definition 7.2, and identity, composition, application, and principal elements are the closure facts the prose announces. **Proof:** yes — identity and principals reduce to decidable inclusion, and composition and application are projections of recursively enumerable sets after reindexing, using surjectivity of the presentations.
